 ## Overview
-Loss masking is a regularization technique in differentiable volume rendering that prevents background pixels from contributing to the training loss [S2]. It solves the problem of networks being forced to match irrelevant default ground-truth values in empty regions, which complicates optimization [S2], and sets itself apart from standard masking by explicitly modulating intermediate feature losses with the output mask [S2] or by enforcing a weight-sum constraint relative to the mask [S3, unofficial]. It builds on [Overall Training Loss](10621-overall-training-loss.md).
+Loss masking is a regularization technique in differentiable volume rendering that prevents background pixels from contributing to the training loss [S2]. It solves the problem of networks being forced to match irrelevant default ground-truth values in empty regions, which complicates optimization [S2]. It sets itself apart from standard masking by explicitly modulating intermediate feature losses with the output mask [S2] or by enforcing a weight-sum constraint relative to the mask [S3, unofficial]. It builds on [Overall Training Loss](10621-overall-training-loss.md) [G].
 
